  "body": "## What changed\n\nThe built-in **Lathe** (`assets/machines/lathe.mch`) and **Mill-turn** (`assets/machines/millturn.mch`) stated a machine-frame Z stroke (`-615..0`, `-620..0`). The limit checker (`app::machine::check_document`) reads travel bands in **program coordinates through the work offset** (#457). With Z0 on the face, every move in front of it was out of stroke: clearance, approach, and facing allowance. No turning program posted on either built-in.\n\n**Reproduced on main before the fix:** a face-and-turn job (Z0 on the finished face, 1 mm facing allowance, clearance Z 5) was refused on both machines, down to the facing approach (`Face1: move 0 reaches 0.800 in Z, the stroke is -615.000..0.000`).\n\nFix: Z is restated as the full stroke each way, the convention PR #477 applies to the VMC and tilt-head. Lathe Z is now `-615..615` and Mill-turn Z is `-620..620`. **X stays one-sided** (`0..320`, `0..650`). A lathe X is measured from the spindle centreline, and the centreline is the program's X0, so `0..R` is already a program-frame band. The Mill-turn Y (`-100..100`) was already symmetric. No checker code changed. This branch is off main, not stacked on #477. It leaves `assets/machines/AGENTS.md` and the module doc alone so the two PRs do not conflict; the convention text is in #477. The decision is in `docs/decisions/493-lathe-z-stroke.md`.\n\n## How tested\n\n- New `crates/app/tests/lathe_stroke.rs`:\n  - `a_turning_job_on_the_built_in_lathe_clears_the_face_and_posts` and `a_turning_job_on_the_built_in_millturn_clears_the_face_and_posts`: Facing plus Profile Rough on a flanged bar, clearance Z 5. `check_document` is clean, `verification_report` has no problems, and `post_setup` posts. Both failed on main.\n  - `a_clearance_past_the_lathe_carriage_still_refuses_by_name` and `a_clearance_past_the_millturn_quill_still_refuses_by_name`: a clearance of L + 20 is refused with `Rough1: move N reaches 635.000 in Z, the stroke is -615.000..615.000` (and the 620 equivalent). There is no X complaint, and the post is refused.\n  - `lathe_x_stays_a_centreline_band`: X stays `0..R` on both machines.\n- These app suites all passed (debug): lathe_stroke, lathe_post, lathe_post_g76, lathe_post_refusals, lathe_field_sim, lathe_field_tools, lathe_spindle_fit, lathe_spindle_keyed, lathe_spindle_law, lathe_cross_drill, turning, turning_profile, turning_bore, turning_holder, turning_blade, turning_contour, turning_contour_stock, turning_form, turning_thread, turning_axial_rapids, turning_stock_approach, turning_insert_zone, drill_prior, drill_prior_kinds, drilling_axial, c_axis_live, coolant_followups, carried_fixtures_cache, conformance, machine, machine_library, posting, setup_mount.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --all-targets -- -D warnings` is clean.\n- Posted G-code is unchanged: no golden NC/CL file changed, and the post scripts read no linear travel.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- I did not run the ui crate suites. The ui crate is untouched. Its tests mount the built-in Lathe by name for door and route checks, but none of them reads the stroke.\n- GUI and the headless MCP path were not run.\n- The Mill-turn X `0..650` also judges its milling ops (live tooling). A milled feature in -X would still read as out of stroke. That is a separate question and is noted in the decision file.\n\nChangelog: The built-in Lathe and Mill-turn machines state their Z stroke in program coordinates (full stroke each way), so turning programs with clearance in front of the face post instead of being refused.\n\nFixes #493\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
